# =============================================================================
# IqraSpace — apps/learning/.env.local.example
#
# Copy this file to `.env.local` (same folder) and fill in real values.
# `.env.local` is gitignored — never commit it. This example file IS committed
# and must only ever contain placeholders, no real secrets.
#
# HOW THIS PROJECT'S CONFIG ACTUALLY FLOWS (read this before editing):
#   1. NEXT_PUBLIC_* vars   -> bundled into the browser by Next.js. Read
#      automatically from this file by `npm run dev` / `npm run build` when
#      run from apps/learning (or via `npm run dev` at the repo root).
#   2. Supabase Edge Function secrets (Deno runtime, google-oauth-exchange /
#      drive-file-proxy) -> a SEPARATE config surface. Next.js does NOT read
#      these from this file. Set them with `supabase secrets set KEY=value`
#      (or Dashboard -> Edge Functions -> Secrets) before
#      `supabase functions deploy`. Listed below for reference only, in the
#      section marked "not read from this file".
#   3. Supabase CLI (`supabase link`, `supabase db push`, run from the repo
#      root) -> reads its own env vars (SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN,
#      SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD) directly from your shell, not from this file,
#      unless you export them first, e.g. from Git Bash:
#        set -a && source apps/learning/.env.local && set +a
#
# Roles: tutor/student/guardian (ordinary accounts, via /signup) plus
# admin/super_admin (platform oversight — never grantable via signup; see
# src/lib/types.ts and the "Super Admin" section below for how they're
# provisioned and what each can do).
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# Application
# =============================================================================

# Local base URL of this Next.js app. Not currently read by any app code
# (routing is relative), but used as the default for APP_URL below and as
# the value to put in Supabase Auth's site_url / additional_redirect_urls
# (supabase/config.toml) and Google's OAuth redirect URI. Optional.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Free-text label, not read by any code. Useful if you run against more than
# one Supabase project locally and want a reminder which one this file
# points at. Optional.
APP_ENV=local

# =============================================================================
# Database (Supabase-managed Postgres)
# =============================================================================
# This app talks to Postgres exclusively through the Supabase REST/Realtime
# API (see Supabase section) — no code here opens a direct Postgres
# connection. The values below are for developers who want to connect a SQL
# client / ORM / migration tool directly (psql, TablePlus, Drizzle, etc.).
# Find them under Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> Database.

# Direct connection (IPv6-only on most plans) — use the pooler host instead
# if your network can't reach it.
SUPABASE_DB_HOST=db.YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
SUPABASE_DB_PORT=5432
SUPABASE_DB_NAME=postgres
SUPABASE_DB_USER=postgres
# Required if you connect directly. This is the project's database password
# (set when the project was created / reset from the Dashboard) — NOT the
# same thing as the anon key or the CLI access token below. Also read
# directly by the Supabase CLI (`supabase link`, `supabase db push`) when
# exported into your shell environment.
SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD=

# Convenience full connection string, built from the fields above. Optional
# — only needed if your tool wants one string instead of discrete fields.
# postgresql://postgres:<SUPABASE_DB_PASSWORD>@db.YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co:5432/postgres
DATABASE_URL=

# =============================================================================
# Supabase
# =============================================================================
# Find these under Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API.

# Project ref (the subdomain in your project URL, e.g. "abcdefghijklmnop").
# Not read by app code; used for `supabase link --project-ref ...` and to
# build the values above/below.
SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF=YOUR-PROJECT-REF

# Read by src/lib/supabaseClient.ts. Required — the app throws loudly at
# import time if either is missing.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.supabase.co
# The publishable/anon key (Supabase's newer key format, `sb_publishable_...`,
# or a legacy `anon` JWT on older projects) — safe to expose to the browser.
# RLS policies (supabase/migrations) are what actually scope access, not
# this key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=your-anon-or-publishable-key

# --- Supabase CLI-only (read from your shell, not from this file) ----------
# A personal access token used ONLY by the `supabase` CLI (`supabase link`,
# `supabase db push`, `supabase functions deploy`) for non-interactive auth.
# This is NOT a service_role key and grants account-level CLI access —
# treat it like a password. Generate one at supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens.
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# --- Server-side only — NEVER prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_, NEVER import into ---
# --- any client-bundled file. Provided automatically to every Edge        ---
# --- Function by the Supabase platform at deploy time — you do not need   ---
# --- to set it yourself for google-oauth-exchange / drive-file-proxy.     ---
# --- The one local consumer is scripts/seed-admin-accounts.mjs            ---
# --- (npm run seed:admins, see "Super Admin" below). Find it under        ---
# --- Supabase Dashboard -> Project Settings -> API ("service_role" key).  ---
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# =============================================================================
# Authentication
# =============================================================================
# Supabase Auth itself is configured in supabase/config.toml (site_url,
# additional_redirect_urls, email confirmation, JWT expiry, rate limits) and
# pushed with `supabase config push` — not via this file. Nothing here
# changes auth behavior; these two are read directly by app code:

# Google OAuth "Connect Google Drive" (architecture §8) — optional, deferred
# feature. Absent -> Materials page correctly shows "not configured" instead
# of a fake connection (see src/app/(app)/materials/page.tsx). The client ID
# is not secret (safe to expose); get both from Google Cloud Console ->
# APIs & Services -> Credentials.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3000/materials

# --- Edge Function secrets (NOT read from this file — see note #2 above) ---
# Set these with `supabase secrets set NAME=value` before deploying
# google-oauth-exchange / drive-file-proxy. Listed here only so every value
# the Google integration needs is documented in one place.
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID              (same value as NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID)
#   GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET          (server-side only — never expose to the browser)
#   GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI           https://YOUR-PROJECT-REF.functions.supabase.co/google-oauth-exchange
#   GOOGLE_TOKEN_ENCRYPTION_KEY   32 random bytes, base64 (e.g. `openssl rand -base64 32`)
#   APP_URL                       base URL to redirect back to after OAuth (defaults to http://localhost:3000)

# =============================================================================
# Demo Accounts
# =============================================================================
# Ordinary tutor/student accounts (not admin/super_admin — see "Super Admin"
# below for those), useful for manual testing and demos. Passwords are never
# stored in the repo — set them locally after creating the account via
# /signup, the Admin -> Manage Users screen, or the Supabase Dashboard.
#
# Login is by USERNAME + password, not email (0019_username_auth.sql) — email
# is optional everywhere. These *_EMAIL vars are kept only as a human-readable
# label for which account is which; log in with the username, not the email.

# The one surviving tutor account after the Phase 3 cleanup (role: tutor,
# username "shaheen") — owns the real "Qaida - Beginners" 52-lesson
# curriculum and its uploaded PDF (see docs/qaida-beginners-curriculum.md).
DEMO_TUTOR_EMAIL=shaheen@iqraspace.com
DEMO_TUTOR_PASSWORD=

# The one surviving student account (role: student, username "student") —
# has no email at all (optional field, left unset), enrolled in the tutor's
# "Quran" class.
DEMO_STUDENT_EMAIL=
DEMO_STUDENT_PASSWORD=

# =============================================================================
# Super Admin
# =============================================================================
# Roles live in public.users.role (0017_admin_super_admin_roles.sql,
# widened to full read/write access in 0018_admin_full_access.sql):
#   'admin'       — full CRUD/management access across the entire app
#                   (users, classes, lessons, curriculum, materials,
#                   progress...), same as 'super_admin' for this startup
#                   phase — the two aren't differentiated yet.
#   'super_admin' — currently identical access to 'admin'.
# Neither role is grantable through public signup (see handle_new_user() in
# that migration) — the only way to create one is:
#   npm run seed:admins
# which reads the four values below (and SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY above) to
# create/update these two accounts directly (usernames "admin"/"superadmin",
# fixed by the script — see scripts/seed-admin-accounts.mjs). Required to
# run that script. Login is by username, not email — see "Demo Accounts"
# above.
#
# GOTCHA: that script runs via `node --env-file=.env.local`, and Node's
# --env-file parser truncates a value at an unquoted `#`, mid-line — e.g.
# ADMIN_PASSWORD=Foo#123 is silently read as just "Foo", so the account ends
# up with a different password than what's in this file. Avoid `#`/`$` in
# these passwords, or wrap the value in double quotes if you do use one:
# ADMIN_PASSWORD="Foo#123"
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-before-running-seed-admins
SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL=superadmin@example.com
SUPER_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-before-running-seed-admins

# Also relevant: Supabase Dashboard account/team membership (manage at
# supabase.com — never an env var) is a separate, platform-level "super
# admin" of sorts (full project access, including bypassing the app
# entirely) — distinct from the in-app super_admin role above.

# =============================================================================
# Feature Flags
# =============================================================================
# No boolean feature-flag system exists in code today. The one flag-like
# behavior that already exists: the Google Drive tab on /materials and the
# Google connection status on /settings are gated on whether
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID is set (see Authentication section above) —
# leave it blank to keep that feature off.

# =============================================================================
# Storage / Files
# =============================================================================
# The lesson-materials Storage bucket (direct PDF upload) is created by
# supabase/migrations/0011_storage_lesson_materials_bucket.sql with a fixed
# name ("lesson-materials") and a fixed 50MB file_size_limit, and referenced
# by the same fixed name in src/lib/storage.ts
# (LESSON_MATERIALS_BUCKET). Neither is env-configurable today — changing
# either means writing a new migration and updating that constant together,
# not just editing this file.

# =============================================================================
# Email / Notifications
# =============================================================================
# In-app notifications (topbar bell, notifications table) need no
# configuration — they're plain DB rows, not email.
#
# Signup/password-reset emails are sent by Supabase Auth's own SMTP, not by
# this app, and are configured in supabase/config.toml
# ([auth.email], [auth.email.smtp]) + `supabase config push` — not here.
# The project currently uses Supabase's shared SMTP (2 emails/hour limit,
# see docs/PROGRESS.md). To switch to a custom SMTP provider, uncomment
# [auth.email.smtp] in supabase/config.toml and set:
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASS=
